DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppcckkbbcc driver handles resource allocation and device attachment for
     the traditional PC/AT keyboard controller, or emulations thereof.  It
     provides up to two logical connections for child devices, the “keyboard”
     slot for a keyboard and the “auxiliary” slot for mice (the latter might
     be missing in older keyboard controllers, or recent emulations).

     To avoid attaching a phantom PS/2 keyboard device, the isa(4) attachment
     of the ppcckkbbcc driver will attempt to detect USB legacy keyboard emulation
     on amd64 and i386 systems.  Unfortunately, the detection heuristics may
     fail on older systems with a real PS/2 keyboard.  The keyboard can be
     forced to attach on these systems, by changing the device flags to 1.

     The acpi(4) attachment of the ppcckkbbcc driver defaults to attach only where
     it would perform better than its legacy isa(4) attachment.  Should this
     logic be insufficient, it is possible to force it to always attach, by
     changing its device flags to 1.

